      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Syndrome characterized by a high parathyroid hormone (PTH) concentration secondary to chronic kidney disease (CKD); results from impaired renal excretion of phosphorus leading to hyperphosphatemia and ionized hypocalcemia, elevation of FGF-23, and suppression of renal calcitriol synthesis.",
              "pdf_page": 778,
              "printed_page": 696,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "In advanced CKD the diminished renal tubular mass produces less calcitriol. Calcitriol exerts negative feedback on PTH synthesis within the parathyroid gland. Low calcitriol, ionized hypocalcemia, and hyperphosphatemia result in increased PTH production and parathyroid gland hyperplasia.",
              "pdf_page": 778,
              "printed_page": 696,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "PTH may act as a uremic toxin, promoting nephrocalcinosis and progression of CKD.",
              "pdf_page": 778,
              "printed_page": 696,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
